From fc075ad98448fa8b1936a0c1527f4711c66b7767 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrey Stoyan <andrey.stoyan.csam@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 01:08:39 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 23/44] feat(chexc): one clause per kind, casts rejected,
 capturing subclasses restate coverage

Follows the revision that narrowed three rules the prototype had implemented from
earlier drafts, and added one:

- One clause of each kind per declaration, like one 'where'; a second is an error
  again. The union of several clauses is gone, and with it the 'throws () throws A'
  error and the '...' repeat rule. A macro that adds requirements merges entries into
  the existing clause, which also preserves the list's inference status.

- A cast or type-pattern target containing a function type is rejected in ANY
  position -- the target itself, a result, a parameter, a tuple component, a type
  argument. Erasure leaves no capability list to check, so a permissive reading would
  launder the requirements of whatever value the cast accepts; the polarity-based
  pessimistic reading shipped yesterday is now an extension, not the base design.
  A parameter-typed target ('x as Box<T>') stays accepted: rejecting it would break
  ordinary generic code, and an instantiation with a function type is an unchecked
  boundary instead. CFunc targets are exempt -- they carry no capabilities.

- Every subclass of a capturing class is itself capturing and its 'captures' list
  must cover the superclass's, each entry covered by an own entry of the same kind.
  This reverses the previous rule (a subclass saw nothing and acquired the
  requirement at the 'super' call); restating keeps the instance's whole handler
  dependency readable in one header and keeps checking local.

- A 'captures' clause takes the entry grammar of a 'throws' clause, so 'captures ()'
  and nested lists parse; a statically empty list means the type is not capturing.
  A capturing type has no implicit default constructor: it must declare an 'init',
  since every constructor carries the captured requirements to its call sites.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
